{% doc %}
  blocks/_highlight-point.liquid

  One thing worth saying about the product: a mark with a number on it, a name,
  and a line under the name.

  ## Why it is not `blocks/badge-highlight.liquid`

  The Highlight tile is a mark, a heading and a line of text in a box, and it
  is the right block for three benefits in a row. Two things make this one
  different, and neither could be a setting on that block.

  It has a **place in the stage**: a column and a row, which only mean anything
  inside `blocks/_highlight-stage.liquid`'s grid. A tile that says "left column,
  row 2" somewhere else is a tile that has escaped its container.

  That grid is also the section's one promise: the product owns the middle
  column, points go either side of it, and no picture at any size can end up
  underneath a label. See the note on **Where it sits** below for what this
  replaced and why.

  And it has a **lit state** that something else drives. The mark fills in when
  `<highlight-points>` puts `data-highlight-active` on it, and what it fills in
  *to* is set once on the stage rather than five times here — see the note
  there.

  ## The number is content

  It is a field rather than something typed into the name, so the order stays
  the order of the blocks, a merchant reordering them does not have to retype
  five numbers, and the sequence is announced to anyone not looking at it. It
  rides on the mark, positioned against it, so switching it on never changes
  the height of the point or moves its neighbours.

  ## The mark is the theme's

  Shape, Ring and the icon colours are `snippets/icon-mark.liquid`: the same
  three groups with the same ids as the Feature icon, the Highlight tile and
  the pills in the other section. A merchant who has drawn one mark in this
  theme has drawn this one.

  Private — the leading underscore — because a point with nothing to be a point
  on is not a block. It is addable inside a **Highlight stage** and nowhere
  else.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign has_icon = false
  if s.icon_source == 'icon' and s.icon != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  capture icon_px
    render 'icon-size', size: s.icon_size, custom: s.icon_size_custom
  endcapture
  assign icon_size = icon_px | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- where it sits ----

    A column and a row, not a pair of coordinates.

    This block used to carry an Across and a Down measured against the stage,
    with the photograph on a layer of its own inside it. Nothing connected the
    two, so a taller bottle or a wider picture put labels across the product
    and the only fix was for somebody to notice and re-drag two sliders.

    Now the stage is a three-column grid and the product owns the middle one.
    A point is in the left column or the right column, so it cannot be over the
    picture at any size — the guarantee is the layout rather than the numbers.
    A **centre** point is for the one above the product, which is row 1 in the
    drawing.

    **Gap from the product** is a margin on the side facing the middle, so it
    only ever moves a point further out. Which side that is comes from the
    column, so one number gives the drawing's symmetric stagger instead of a
    left value and a right value that have to be kept equal by hand.

    Emitted on every breakpoint and read on one: `assets/base.css` drops the
    placement below 990px, where the points are a plain grid under the picture.
    Writing it anyway is what lets a merchant lay out the desktop while the
    editor is previewing a phone.
  endcomment
  assign column = s.column | default: 'left'
  assign row = s.row | default: 2 | plus: 0
  assign offset = s.offset | default: 40 | plus: 0

  comment
    **Nudge up or down**, and why it is not a second row setting.

    A row is as tall as the tallest point in it — a mark, a name and a line —
    and the drawing does not stack its rows that way: the upper pair sits level
    with the top of the picture, beside the first point's caption rather than
    below it. No number of rows produces that, because a row short enough to
    give it would cut the point above off.

    So the row keeps the point's full height and this moves the drawn result
    out of it, as a `translate` in `assets/base.css`. A margin would be read
    when the row is measured and would drag every row after it along; this
    changes nothing the stage measures, which is what keeps the scroll run
    reading the same box.

    Written for every point and honoured for two: a centre point is in the
    product's column and a centre point nudged downward is the one way left to
    put a label on the picture. The stylesheet applies it to left and right
    only, and the schema says so.
  endcomment
  assign nudge = s.nudge | default: 0 | plus: 0

  case s.row_align
    when 'center'
      assign row_align = 'center'
    when 'end'
      assign row_align = 'end'
    else
      assign row_align = 'start'
  endcase

  case s.alignment
    when 'start'
      assign align_axis = 'start'
      assign text_align = 'start'
    when 'end'
      assign align_axis = 'end'
      assign text_align = 'end'
    else
      assign align_axis = 'center'
      assign text_align = 'center'
  endcase

  assign preset = s.preset | default: 'default'
  assign title_classes = 'highlight-point__title block-text--' | append: preset
  if s.color == 'gradient'
    assign title_classes = title_classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  endif

  comment
    Presence, not a value: `assets/highlight-points.js` reads the attribute with
    `hasAttribute`, and an unchecked box has to emit nothing at all rather than
    `data-highlight-active="false"`.

    With the scroll run switched on this is only the starting state — the
    component takes it over on the first frame. With the run off it is the
    whole answer, and it is also what a customer sees if the module never
    arrives.
  endcomment
  assign is_active = s.active | default: false
-%}

{%- capture icon_mark_style -%}
  {%- render 'icon-mark', block: s, declarations: true -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture title_style -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: s -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture point_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --hp-row: {{ row }};
  --hp-row-align: {{ row_align }};
  --hp-offset: {{ offset }}px;
  --hp-nudge: {{ nudge }}px;
  --hp-align: {{ align_axis }};
  --hp-text-align: {{ text_align }};
  --hp-width: {{ s.width | default: 150 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --hp-gap: {{ s.gap | default: 16 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --hp-stack-gap: {{ s.gap_vertical | default: 2 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --hp-text-size: {{ s.text_size | default: 16 | plus: 0 | divided_by: 10.0 }}rem;
  {%- if s.text_color != blank -%}
    --hp-text-color: {{ s.text_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  --hp-number-size: {{ s.number_size | default: 28 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- if s.number_background != blank -%}
    --hp-number-background: {{ s.number_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.number_color != blank -%}
    --hp-number-color: {{ s.number_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `data-highlight-point` is what the component collects on, and it is a data
  attribute rather than the class so a restyle cannot silently take the
  behaviour with it. `data-column` is the same idea for the layout: the grid
  placement in `assets/base.css` is keyed on it, so the column a point is in is
  one value read by both the stylesheet and anyone reading the markup.

  A `<div>` and not an `<li>`. The points have to be direct children of the
  stage for the grid to place them — a `<ul>` between the two would be one grid
  item holding all five, and the column that keeps them off the product would
  not exist. `display: contents` on the list would flatten it, but an element
  with no box is one an IntersectionObserver never sees, and the entrance
  animation depends on that.

  What is lost is the "list of 5" a screen reader would announce. What is kept
  is every point's number, name and line, each read in order — which is the
  content. The layout guarantee was worth more than the count.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  class="highlight-point icon-mark-host"
  data-highlight-point
  data-column="{{ column }}"
  {% if is_active %}data-highlight-active aria-current="true"{% endif %}
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ point_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if has_icon -%}
    <span class="highlight-point__mark icon-mark" aria-hidden="true" style="{{ icon_mark_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
      {% render 'icon-mark', block: s %}
      {% render 'icon', icon: s.icon, size: icon_size %}

      {%- comment -%}
        Positioned against the mark, which `.icon-mark` already makes a
        containing block, and out of flow — so a point with a number and one
        without are the same height and sit on the same line in the grid.

        `aria-hidden="false"` back on, because the mark around it is hidden and
        the number is not decoration: it is this point's place in the run.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if s.number != blank -%}
        <span class="highlight-point__number" aria-hidden="false">{{ s.number | escape }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </span>
  {%- elsif s.number != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      No glyph, so the number is the mark. Same element, same settings, drawn
      on its own and back in the flow.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span class="highlight-point__number highlight-point__number--alone">{{ s.number | escape }}</span>
  {%- endif -%}

  <span class="highlight-point__body">
    {%- if s.title != blank -%}
      <span class="{{ title_classes }}" style="{{ title_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
        {{- s.title | escape -}}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if s.text != blank -%}
      <span class="highlight-point__text">{{ s.text }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </span>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% # theme-check-disable ExcessiveSettingsCount %}
